WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.324
True position, part five. Threaded holes,

2
00:00:04.424 --> 00:00:07.080
and the complications they bring.

3
00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:14.335
Here's a section through a tapped hole, three eighths sixteen

4
00:00:14.435 --> 00:00:18.453
U N C, with a position callout of fifteen thousandths.

5
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.553
A thread has three diameters. The major diameter at the roots,

6
00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:26.458
the minor diameter at the crests, and the pitch diameter in

7
00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:30.430
between, where the thread and the groove are the same width.

8
00:00:30.516 --> 00:00:34.803
By default, position on a thread applies to the axis of the

9
00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:40.156
pitch cylinder. That's in Y fourteen point five, section two point nine.

10
00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.661
To control a different diameter, the print adds a note under the frame: major

11
00:00:45.761 --> 00:00:50.100
dia, or minor dia. The same goes for a thread used as a datum.

12
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.819
Here's the first problem. A C M M probe can't touch the pitch

13
00:00:55.919 --> 00:01:00.590
cylinder. It only lands on the crests and flanks of the thread.

14
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:05.730
So inspectors screw in a thread gauge pin. Its precision ground shank

15
00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.683
sticks out, and the C M M measures the shank instead.

16
00:01:09.783 --> 00:01:13.841
But the pin's own thread has clearance, so it can tip and shift

17
00:01:13.941 --> 00:01:18.263
a little. Any tip gets bigger the farther up the shank you measure.

18
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:22.185
Use snug-fitting pins, seat them the same way every time,

19
00:01:22.285 --> 00:01:25.500
and measure the shank close to the part surface.

20
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.786
The tap can also run slightly off the drilled hole. Then the

21
00:01:30.886 --> 00:01:35.740
drilled minor diameter and the thread itself don't share the same axis.

22
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:39.456
So probing the minor diameter measures where the drill

23
00:01:39.556 --> 00:01:42.628
went, not where the thread is. That's only the

24
00:01:42.728 --> 00:01:45.593
right answer when the print says minor dia.

25
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:50.770
The second problem is tilt. A bolt follows the thread's

26
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.263
axis, and it sticks up through the mating part.

27
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.467
With a normal position callout, the zone only

28
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:01.115
exists inside the threaded hole. This tilted thread

29
00:02:01.215 --> 00:02:04.190
stays inside it, top to bottom, and passes.

30
00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:08.065
But follow the axis up through the mating part. The tilt

31
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:12.085
keeps growing with height. If the mating hole is also near

32
00:02:12.185 --> 00:02:15.413
the edge of its own zone, the bolt runs into it.

33
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.829
The fix is a projected tolerance zone: a circled P,

34
00:02:19.929 --> 00:02:24.760
followed by the projected height, here five hundred thousandths.

35
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.610
The zone moves out of the hole, and sits above

36
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.280
it, where the bolt actually passes through the mating part.

37
00:02:31.366 --> 00:02:35.141
Now check the axis at the top of the projected zone. Delta X

38
00:02:35.241 --> 00:02:40.114
is nine thousandths there, and position is eighteen point four. The same part

39
00:02:40.214 --> 00:02:44.376
fails, and that's the right answer, because the bolt wouldn't fit.

40
00:02:44.466 --> 00:02:48.307
The projected height should be at least the maximum thickness of the

41
00:02:48.407 --> 00:02:51.610
mating part, or the height of a stud. Some prints show it

42
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.666
instead with a heavy chain line and a dimension beside the centerline.

43
00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:01.465
The third problem: material modifiers on a thread. If the

44
00:03:01.565 --> 00:03:04.940
frame says M M C, the bonus comes from the

45
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.580
pitch diameter, not from a simple hole size.

46
00:03:08.666 --> 00:03:13.686
The pitch diameter tolerance is small, and the actual pitch diameter is hard

47
00:03:13.786 --> 00:03:17.390
to measure on a C M M. A torqued bolt also pulls itself

48
00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.836
to the center of the thread. So there's little real bonus to gain.

49
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:26.847
Unless you're checking it with a threaded functional gauge, the safe practice

50
00:03:26.947 --> 00:03:29.843
is to treat a tapped hole's position as R F S.

51
00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:36.180
Where does a number like fifteen thousandths come from? Y fourteen point

52
00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:40.918
five gives two formulas in its appendix, one for a screw threaded

53
00:03:41.018 --> 00:03:43.833
into a part, and one for a bolt and nut.

54
00:03:43.916 --> 00:03:49.534
For a fixed fastener, each part gets half the difference between the clearance

55
00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:52.832
hole at M M C and the fastener at M M C. Four

56
00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:58.916
oh six minus three seventy-five, divided by two, is about fifteen thousandths each.

57
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.623
For a floating fastener, both parts have clearance, so

58
00:04:02.723 --> 00:04:06.760
each part gets the whole difference: thirty-one thousandths.

59
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:11.577
The fixed fastener formula assumes the threaded hole is square to

60
00:04:11.677 --> 00:04:16.330
the joint. That's exactly why tapped holes get a projected zone.

61
00:04:17.516 --> 00:04:20.707
To sum up threaded holes: position is on the

62
00:04:20.807 --> 00:04:24.596
pitch diameter axis unless the print says otherwise.

63
00:04:24.683 --> 00:04:29.193
Projected zones check the axis where the bolt really goes.

64
00:04:29.283 --> 00:04:32.852
Measure with a snug gauge pin, close to the surface,

65
00:04:32.952 --> 00:04:35.393
and don't count on bonus from M M C.

66
00:04:35.483 --> 00:04:39.758
And always check the numbers against the print, and the edition of the

67
00:04:39.858 --> 00:04:43.883
standard it calls out. That's the end of the true position series.
